---
interface Props {
  /** The art itself, imported with `?raw`. One line per row of the grid. */
  art: string;
  /** What a screen reader announces in place of several thousand symbols. */
  label: string;
  class?: string;
}

/*
  The rest of the props are spread onto the wrapper for one reason: Astro
  scopes a component's styles with a data-astro-cid attribute, and a class
  handed down from a parent arrives here as a plain string with no attribute
  attached to it. Without the spread the parent's own rule for this element
  matches nothing, silently, and the layout it describes never happens.
*/
const { art, label, class: className, ...rest } = Astro.props;

/*
  The column count is read off the art instead of written down anywhere,
  because the photo gets re-sampled and the grid changes width when it does.
  CSS divides the available width by this number, so the block lands on its
  container exactly whatever the file happens to be that day.

  Carriage returns come off first. core.autocrlf is on for this repo, so a
  fresh checkout on Windows hands the file back with CRLF endings, and every
  row would measure one column wider than it draws.

  The trailing newline comes off too. <pre> renders it as a real extra row,
  and the picture's height is exactly its row count.

  The leading one is put back deliberately, and it is not decoration. An HTML
  parser drops a single newline straight after a <pre> start tag, so when the
  art opens on blank rows, as it does whenever the subject does not reach the
  top of the frame, the first of them is eaten: the block measured 74 rows
  against the file's 75 and the whole picture rode a row high. The sentinel is
  what the parser eats instead.
*/
const rows = art.replace(/\r/g, "").replace(/\n+$/, "").split("\n");
const cols = Math.max(...rows.map((row) => row.length));
const grid = `\n${rows.join("\n")}`;

/*
  role="img" with a label is the whole accessibility story here. Without the
  pair, a screen reader reads several thousand punctuation marks out loud, one
  at a time; with it the element announces once, as the picture it is. The
  audit fails the build on a role="img" that has lost its aria-label, because
  nothing a browser can show you would ever reveal it.
*/
---

<div class:list={["ascii", className]} {...rest}>
  <pre
    class="ascii__grid"
    role="img"
    aria-label={label}
    style={`--ascii-cols: ${cols}`}
  >{grid}</pre>
</div>

{/*
  These live here rather than in global.css so that nothing ships while nothing
  renders. Astro emits a component's scoped styles only when the component is
  used, and the portrait is currently not on any page, so this block costs the
  build zero bytes until someone imports it again. It also travels with the
  component, which is the point of keeping it drop-in.
*/}
<style>
  /*
    A photograph redrawn as a grid of the characters - / = * # @ $. It is
    something you read, not something you can act on, so it takes the amber
    ramp and nothing about it is blue.

    The type is sized from the grid rather than the grid from the type. The
    wrapper is the query container, 100cqw is its content box, and the line
    below divides that by the number of cells in a row. Whatever column count
    the file arrives at, the art fills its column exactly and never sets the
    width itself, which is also why the wrapper can shrink to nothing on a
    narrow screen instead of pushing the page sideways.

    0.6 is IBM Plex Mono's advance as a fraction of the em. It was measured off
    a rendered <pre> at 16px, 100px and 1000px, and separately for each of the
    seven glyphs the art is drawn from, in case one of them fell through to a
    fallback face: every reading came back 0.600000. It was corroborated at the
    time against the site's old 68ch prose cap, which resolved to 693.6px at
    17px, a rule that no longer exists; the advance itself is a property of the
    face and is unaffected. Rendered, the sum lands where it should: in a 384px column the art
    drew 383.719px of ink and its own scrollWidth never exceeded its client
    width at any viewport tested.

    line-height has to be exactly 1 and letter-spacing exactly 0. The photo was
    sampled on cells 0.6 wide by 1.0 tall; any other line height stretches the
    face, and any tracking inherited from an ancestor widens every row past the
    width the font size was solved for.
  */
  .ascii {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  .ascii__grid {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: calc(100cqw / (var(--ascii-cols) * 0.6));
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    white-space: pre;
    color: var(--amber);
  }

  /*
    Not printed. The art is lit glyphs on a dark ground, so a denser symbol
    means a brighter pixel. Ink on paper runs the other way and it would come
    out as its own negative.
  */
  @media print {
    .ascii {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
